body:
Dear Monitoring Officer,

I request an internal review of the Council's response to my Environmental
Information Regulations 2004 request (reference 00210772).

Thank you for the response issued on 7 July 2026. I appreciate the time
taken to consider my request and the searches undertaken. However, having
carefully reviewed the response alongside the earlier correspondence, I do
not believe my clarified request has been fully addressed, nor do I believe
the response adequately explains the scope of the searches that were
undertaken.

Clarification process
On 28 May 2026 the Council advised that the relevant service area required
clarification regarding the nature and reason for my enquiry.

I provided that clarification on 7 June 2026.
On 9 June 2026 I was informed that my clarification had been forwarded to
the Planning Department for consideration.
Given that sequence, I reasonably expected the final response to address
the full scope of my clarified request. Instead, the response appears to
have focused primarily upon identifying two planning applications and
referring me to the Planning Register.
Those applications are relevant, but they represent only one aspect of the
information requested.

Scope of my request
My request concerns environmental information relating to the historic
Great House Farm (Ty Mawr) estate, including land now forming Church View
Close and adjoining land on the north side of Leckwith Road.

The request extends from approximately 1875 onwards where records exist.
It includes, where held:

planning records;
officer reports;
committee reports and minutes;
planning consultation responses;
planning correspondence;
environmental reports;
archaeological reports;
heritage assessments;
demolition records;
redevelopment records;
engineering drawings;
highways information;
drainage and sewer records;
GIS and mapping datasets;
aerial photography;
environmental assessments;
land-related correspondence;
estate management records;
property records;
archived files;

and any other environmental information falling within Regulation 2(1) of
the Environmental Information Regulations 2004.

Matters that remain unanswered
Although the response identifies two planning applications, it does not
explain whether information was sought or considered in relation to several
parts of my request.

In particular, it does not explain whether searches were undertaken for:

archived paper planning files;
committee reports and minutes;
officer reports;
consultation responses;
supporting technical documents;
demolition records relating to Great House Farm;
archaeological reports held by the Council;
heritage assessments;
environmental assessments;
engineering reports;
highways records;
drainage records;
GIS datasets;
correspondence relating to redevelopment;
records inherited from predecessor authorities;
records transferred to Glamorgan Archives or any other repository.

Adequacy of the searches
To assist my understanding of the Council's response, I would be grateful
if the internal review could explain:

Which Council departments were asked to search.
Which electronic record systems were searched.
Which paper archives were searched.
Whether off-site archived records were searched.
Whether records inherited from predecessor authorities were searched.
Whether any GIS databases were searched.
Whether searches extended beyond the Planning Register itself.
Whether search terms or keywords were used and, if so, what they were.
Whether date restrictions were applied.
Whether any information was identified but withheld.

Information not held
Where the Council concludes that information is not held, I respectfully
request that the review explains:
which reasonable searches were undertaken before reaching that conclusion;
whether the information never existed;
whether it was transferred elsewhere;
whether it was destroyed;
if destroyed, the applicable retention schedule and date of destruction;
if transferred, the destination repository.
Information withheld

If any information has been withheld under the Environmental Information
Regulations, I request that the review identifies:
the precise Regulation relied upon;
the reasons why that exception applies;
where required, the public interest considerations;
whether partial disclosure or redaction was considered.

Planning Register
The response relies upon Regulation 6(1)(b) on the basis that planning
information is publicly available.

I would therefore appreciate clarification as to whether the online
Planning Register contains the complete planning files, including:

officer reports;
committee reports;
consultation responses;
internal memoranda;
correspondence;
amendments;
supporting plans;
technical reports;
conditions;
enforcement material (if any);
archived documents.
If it does not, I request disclosure of any remaining environmental
information held by the Council.
GIS information

The response states that my request for GIS information lacked sufficient
detail.
For the avoidance of doubt, I seek copies of any GIS datasets or historic
mapping held by the Council relating to Great House Farm/Ty Mawr, including
where available:

parcel boundaries;
planning polygons;
historic base mapping;
aerial imagery;
highway extents;
rights of way;
drainage assets;
land referencing;
scanned plans attached to GIS records.
Personal data
Separately, to the extent that any records contain my own personal data, I
request that this element of my request also be treated as a Subject Access
Request under Article 15 UK GDPR.

Closing
I have sought throughout this process to clarify and narrow my request
wherever asked to do so. My intention is simply to understand the complete
environmental and administrative record held by the Council concerning
Great House Farm/Ty Mawr.

If the Council has conducted comprehensive searches, I would be grateful if
the internal review could explain those searches in sufficient detail to
demonstrate that all reasonable steps have been taken. If further
information is held, I request that it be disclosed unless a specific
statutory exception applies.
Thank you for undertaking the internal review. I look forward to receiving
the outcome.

Regards,

Sion Buckler (Ret'd Sgt)
Royal Signals Cyber & Electronic Warfare Professional & Life Member
02031377118

P.S. For the avoidance of doubt, where the Council holds environmental
information relating to the historic Great House Farm/Ty Mawr estate prior
to the 1989–1992 redevelopment—including records connected with the 1938
auction of the Cowbridge and Llandough Great House Farm estate, subsequent
ownership, occupation, boundary changes, estate management, landscaping,
trees, rights of way, demolition, redevelopment or associated land
management—I request that such information also be included within the
scope of this review.

In particular, if the Council holds any recorded information concerning the
removal of a commemorative tree formerly situated at or associated with
Great House Farm, including work orders, contractor instructions,
arboricultural reports, highways or grounds maintenance records,
photographs, correspondence, GIS records or file notes, I request
disclosure of those records. If no such information is held, I would be
grateful if the Council could confirm whether it has ever held such
records, whether they have been transferred or destroyed, or whether
another public authority is likely to hold them.

Finally, if, during the course of the internal review, it becomes apparent
that any part of my request has been interpreted too narrowly, I ask that
this letter be treated as further clarification rather than a new request,
so that all relevant recorded information held by the Council can be
identified and considered for disclosure under the Environmental
Information Regulations 2004.

Dear Ms Longford,

Thank you for your response and for the time taken to clarify Cadw's current position regarding ATISN 27021. I note your confirmation that:

Great House Farm was inspected for statutory listing on 29 July 1988; the two photographs formed part of that listing assessment; the "YYY" code on the reverse of the photographs indicated that the building did not meet the listing criteria; no official listing file has been located; and apart from the two photographs, no further records have been identified. I am grateful for those clarifications.

As this request is being considered under the Environmental Information Regulations 2004 (and, where applicable, the Freedom of Information Act 2000), and bearing in mind the EIR's presumption in favour of disclosure, I would be grateful for one further clarification regarding the scope of the searches undertaken.

Contemporary newspaper reports from December 1988 indicate that Cadw was actively engaged with a number of parties during its consideration of Great House Farm. These reports refer to:

Cadw considering emergency ("spot") listing;
discussions with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales; comments made by a Cadw spokesperson explaining why the building did not satisfy the listing criteria; and public statements regarding the architectural and archaeological significance of the site.

Given those contemporary reports, could you please confirm whether the searches undertaken for ATISN 27021 specifically included records such as:

internal memoranda;
inspector-to-management correspondence;
briefing notes;
file notes and telephone notes;
meeting records;
press office files, media briefings or press releases;
correspondence with the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales; correspondence with the Welsh Office; correspondence with the local planning authority, conservation officers or other public bodies; ministerial submissions or briefing papers; and any other correspondence, whether internal or external, relating to the consideration of Great House Farm for statutory listing.

The public interest in ensuring that all environmental information has been identified is considerable. Great House Farm was a site with a recorded history extending over approximately 800 years, associated with the historic manor of Llandough, incorporating significant historic fabric including the coach house and outbuildings, and lying within the wider context of the Marconi wireless experiments at Lavernock. It had also been the subject of a 1974 public petition concerning its preservation and, as confirmed by Cadw, was sufficiently significant in 1988 to warrant consideration for emergency ("spot") listing. These factors underline the importance of establishing whether any additional environmental information relating to Cadw's assessment survives.

Finally, if the official listing file and/or any associated correspondence once existed but are no longer held, I would be grateful if you could clarify Cadw's records management position by confirming:
whether any retention schedule applied to those records;

whether any authorised destruction took place;
whether any destruction certificate, disposal log or other records management documentation exists; if no such documentation exists, whether this is because no destruction record was created or because any such record is itself no longer held.

My intention is simply to establish whether any further environmental information relating to Cadw's consideration of Great House Farm survives beyond the two inspection photographs already disclosed. Thank you again for your assistance, and I look forward to your response.

Yours sincerely,

Sion Buckler

FOI Team,

Thank you for your email concerning FOI:2026-036.

I do not agree that FOI:2026-032 should be closed or treated as replaced.

The three matters are distinct and should remain so.

Internal review of FOI 2026-024
This remains a review of how the original 27 July request was interpreted and searched. I confirm it is separate. Nothing in FOI:2026-036 is a comment on that review.

FOI 2026-032 (your letter of 26 August 2026; also written by you as FOI 2026-32) This is the request you yourselves extracted from my 26 August email. Its subject is the records your searches for FOI 2026-024 had already identified: the “other archaeological discoveries recorded at Great House Farm (Ty Mawr), Llandough” and the Church Farm, Llandough finds which you excluded from 2026-024 as out of scope. You registered that as a new request and gave a response date of 23 September 2026. That request has not been withdrawn. Those records have already been located. They do not require a new collection-wide trawl.

FOI:2026-036 (my email of 8 September 2026). This is a new request. It is not a restatement of 2026-032. It has additional limbs:

a schedule of every object, archive file, accession, loan, enquiry or correspondence record indexed, provenanced or otherwise recorded as from Great House Farm / Ty Mawr / Llandough Farm;

the Church Farm schedule only so far as already retrieved in the 2026-024 search, and without a new full trawl;

predecessor accession, register, day-book, donations-book and correspondence searches for 1875–1915 (Cardiff Museum / Welsh Museum of Natural History / National Museum of Wales) for ironwork, armour, visor, lance, helmet, “armour from Llandough”, or a find from Great House / Llandough Farm;

object records for Llandough ironwork not labelled “visor” or “lance” but associated with the farmhouse floor or rear wing / dairy.

If it assists you administratively, I have no objection to a single letter that answers both 2026-032 and 2026-036, provided that:
a. FOI 2026-032 is not closed, withdrawn or treated as superseded;

b. the records already identified in the 2026-024 search are scheduled and disclosed as a discrete part of that letter, and that part is provided by the date you gave for 2026-032 (23 September 2026);

c. the additional limbs of 2026-036 remain live and are answered on the 2026-036 timescale;

d. the two requests are not aggregated so as to refuse either under section 12;
e. the internal review of FOI 2026-024 continues independently.

The purpose of 2026-036 was to obtain the records you had already found and set aside, together with the predecessor-register and full-site schedule work that 2026-024 did not cover. It was not an invitation to extinguish 2026-032 or to restart the clock on material already retrieved.

Please confirm that FOI 2026-032 remains open on the terms above.

Regards,

Sion Buckler
[redacted]

Mr Haste,

I refer to your letter of 30 July 2026 (N610CGV) and to my emails of 26 May, 2 June, 3 June (clarification, physical inspection, and preservation), 30 July and 12 August 2026.

This is not a complaint and not a request to reopen the stage 2 review concluded by Mr Gill on 25 May 2026. It is a request that HM Land Registry answer the factual questions already put, which remain unanswered. Your 30 July letter listed documents still held. It did not answer what those documents contain, nor the earlier questions that listing was meant to enable.
Please answer each of the following. If a point cannot be answered without reading a listed item, say so and name the item number.

1. 1971 abstract of title
Earlier correspondence referred to a 1971 abstract lodged with first registration. It does not appear as a separate item in your 30 July schedule. Confirm whether it:
survives as a separate document;
forms part of a listed item (give the title and item number); or

is no longer held.

2. “No question or doubt” certificate
Earlier correspondence referred to a certificate that the applicant’s solicitors were “not aware of any question or doubt affecting the title.” Confirm whether that wording appears in Form 1E dated 30 November 1982 (WA231076 item 13), in another surviving document (identify it), or is no longer held.

3. Marriage certificate relied on by Mr Gill
Mr Gill’s letter of 25 May 2026 stated that identity was established because “there is a marriage certificate to show this.” Confirm whether that certificate:

was lodged on the first-registration file;
is one of the items listed on 30 July (give number); or
is not held by HM Land Registry and was asserted from some other source.

4. Contents of the surviving first-registration records
Confirm whether any of the documents listed on 30 July 2026 for WA231076 or WA240304 contain any reference to:
Mary Williams / Mary Buckler;
Fred Buckler;
William (Billy) Buckler;
Ty Mawr / Great House Farm, farmhouse, garden or curtilage;
any person other than the applicant asserting ownership, occupation or another interest;

possession proceedings, litigation, objection, caution, competing claim, or any question or doubt as to title.
If any such reference exists, identify the title and item number. If none exists in the surviving records, confirm that fact in writing.

5. Physical check
On 3 June 2026 I asked to inspect the physical files, or scans if the originals are not accessible. That request has not been answered. Please confirm:

whether the originals of the 30 July items still exist in physical form;

where they are held;

the procedure, identification and fee for inspection; and

if originals have been destroyed or are not produced for inspection, the date and manner of destruction or the reason inspection is refused.

6. Form OC2
To avoid an unnecessarily broad application, identify the item numbers from your 30 July schedule that comprise the surviving first-registration record, in particular:

Form 1E (WA231076 item 13);
A13-DL list of documents lodged (item 17);
19 November 1982 conveyance (item 4);
Linklaters & Paines correspondence (WA231076 items 16 and 18; WA240304 items 4, 5 and 7);

Form 1C and 23 May 1975 conveyance (WA240304 items 11 and 12); A28 / draft entries;

the 1971 abstract, if it survives;
any examiner’s notes, requisitions, memoranda or file notes that survive. If any of those categories do not survive, say which.

7. Preservation
Your 30 July letter stated the listed documents “will remain on file.” Please confirm that none of the WA231076 or WA240304 first-registration material, including scans or microfiche, will be destroyed while these questions and any OC2 application remain outstanding.

I asked these questions so that HM Land Registry would either confirm the contents of its own listed records or state that it will not examine them. A further inventory, a direction to Form OC2, or a restatement that the complaints procedure is closed is not an answer.

I would be grateful for a numbered reply to points 1–7.

Yours sincerely,

Sion Buckler (Ret'd Sergeant)
Royal Signals Cyber/Electronic Warfare Professional and Life Member N610CGV — WA231076 / WA240304
